# {{ if .DisplayName }}{{ .DisplayName }}{{ else }}{{ .Container }}{{ end }}
{{ if eq .Type "worker" }}
The `{{ .Container }}` worker of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}, in project {{ .Project }}: a
long-running process in Python 3.12 or later, managed with [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)
and scaffolded by C4 Factory.

## Run it

```sh
uv run python -m app   # the image runs python -m app; Ctrl-C stops it
```

It does a round of work every 10 seconds and logs to stderr, one line per event.
SIGTERM or SIGINT lets the round in progress finish, then the worker exits 0 within a
tenth of a second. A round that raises is logged with its traceback, and the wait
before the next one doubles with each failure in a row, up to 5 minutes.
{{- if eq .Deployment "k8s-manifests" }} On
Kubernetes, SIGTERM is how a pod is stopped, so a round must finish within the pod's
grace period (30 seconds unless the Deployment says otherwise).
{{- end }}
{{ else if eq .Type "job" }}
The `{{ .Container }}` job of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}, in project {{ .Project }}: a program
in Python 3.12 or later that runs once to completion, managed with
[uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) and scaffolded by C4 Factory.

## Run it

```sh
uv run python -m app   # the image runs python -m app
```

It does its work once, logs to stderr, one line per event, and exits: 0 when done, 1
when the work raised, with the traceback in the log. It never loops or waits for a
signal the way a worker does; work that must go on is a worker.
{{- if eq .Deployment "k8s-manifests" }} On
Kubernetes a Job runs it, and a run that exits non-zero runs again, up to the Job's
`backoffLimit` (3), so a run must be safe to redo.
{{- end }}
{{ else }}
The `{{ .Container }}` command-line program of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}, in project {{ .Project }}:
a command in Python 3.12 or later, managed with [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) and
scaffolded by C4 Factory.

## Use it

```sh
